00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:16.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-They don't.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:20.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, the premise
of this one's, I think --

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It is a nice, specific brand.
-It is.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:24.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
It basically --
The premise of the podcast

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.385 line:92%
is that everything is a brand.

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:28.154
Every person, every politician
every product, every company

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:29.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
every religion,
every institution.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
Anybody with a Facebook page
is a brand.

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
So every week,
I break down what are, kind of

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
I see as the ten brands
of the week.

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.529 size:81% position:41%
It could be a person.
It could be a company.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.865
And then I interview a big brand
in and of themselves.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:41.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I've done
Michael J. Fox and Don Lemon

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Denis Leary
is dropping tomorrow.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I got Hunter Biden
next week.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we're doing a lot of --
a lot of fun people.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's interesting.
-And, now, this --

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have a background
in advertising.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
And is this
how you first started

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:53.480 line:92%
seeing everything as a brand

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.280 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-Yeah, I -- That's --

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.583
I've kind of had
an identity crisis in my career.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've done a bunch
of different things.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:01.888
So, for about 25 years, I ra
an ad agency, built brands

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:04.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then for the last 15 years
on television,

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been kind of
analyzing political brands

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:09.629
and, you know, every brand
way -- which way, you know

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
kind of aspect in life,
where everything is going in

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.200 size:78% position:39%
So kind of everything
has come together.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.002 line:92%
-Certainly, no one in politics

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
is more of a brand
than Donald Trump.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it strikes me
that, right now,

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:21.741 line:92%
the GOP is having trouble

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:24.043 align:start size:94% position:6%
being a brand independent
of Donald Trump.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:26.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like that's even
an effort they're making?

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:29.682
And do you think it's an effor
that, if you were in charge,

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:30.750 size:81% position:41% line:92%
they should be making?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What I don't understand
about the GOP --

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because they are Donald Trump,
and beyond that,

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
they're not --
they're the party of "no."

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:37.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
The party of resentment.
They don't stand for anything.

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
What I don't get
is how dumb it is

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:42.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
because Trump is
a losing proposition right now

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:44.230 line:92%
He's got 75% of the party,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
which translates
to 30% of the population.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.501 line:92%
He's not electable again.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.802 line:92%
He cannot get any swing voters

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:54.507
So what I don't get is why
they're so beholden to him

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.476 size:78% position:39% line:92%
in that it's a loser.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's an absolute loser
going forward.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.546
And that's beyond
the indictments that are coming.

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:01.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, you talked about it
earlier in the show

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
with my old buddy
Michael Cohen --

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.117 size:75% position:38% line:92%
about Michael Cohen.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:05.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it's a loser.
But the party is lost.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:08.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
They've fallen down,
and they can't get back up

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.390 line:92%
-Now, I want to ask, as well

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.560 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the mayoral race
that's heating up in New York.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
You had actually
at one time in your life

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:17.697 line:92%
considered running for mayor

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.566 size:78% position:39%
What made you decide
that was not for you?

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:21.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Um, I would have loved
to run for mayor.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.203
I really think I could have been
a great mayor.

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:24.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
I love the city.
I grew up in Queens.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:26.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
I could raise money.
I'm a centrist.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just think, you know,
putting my family through it -

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, it's a --
Donald Trump was a unicorn

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:32.912
Everybody goes, "Well,
Trump ran, and, you know,

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
he had all this stuff."
That was part of his brand

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:36.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
he was Teflon in that way.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:38.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
And not that I've done
anything horrible,

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:42.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I have led my life
as a colorful private citizen,

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:45.058
and I just didn't need
all that color out there.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.259 line:92%
So I took a pass. I took a pass.

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.228 size:81% position:41%
-Do you think New York
right now needs a --

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:50.396
You talked about being fro
New York and loving New York

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think New York's mayo
right now

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
has to be a cheerleader
for the city?

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think that enthusiasm
for the city

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:57.270 line:92%
is what's needed post-pandemic

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Has to be a cheerleader,
has to be a builder,

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:02.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
and has to juggle in that
on the one sense,

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:03.977 size:88% position:44% line:92%
it's a progressive city,

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:06.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
but on the other sense,
people want to be protected.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.014 line:92%
And they still, you know -

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we're not
a "defund the police" city

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's a city
of businesspeople also,

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:16.155 line:92%
and it's a city of everything.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you need someone,
I think, down the middle.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:21.694
Can't go too far left. Certainly
can't go too far right.

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:22.929 line:92%
We operate best down the middle.

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Let me ask about the bran
of New York City

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:28.067 size:84% position:42%
and how it has not been
effective for people

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.771
with presidential ambitions,
in that Giuliani, Bloomberg,

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.707
de Blasio all thought, "Hey,
if I could be mayor of New York,

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.110
I'd make a great president,"
and it seems like the rest

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the country said,
"No, thank you."

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:40.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think that's based
on the candidate

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
or based on the fact
that the rest of the country

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
is less enamored with
what it takes to run New York?

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:45.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think
those are the candidates.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, Bobby Kennedy
was a senator from New York,

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:50.456 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he would have won,
you know, I'm sure,

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.391 line:92%
had that tragedy not happened.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it always comes down
to the human being.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:54.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think
the country goes, "We --"

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:57.463
Look. We elected a peanut farmer
from Plains, Georgia.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:01.234 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we elected a governo
who was an actor.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's the person.
I don't think New York

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
has a stigma --
"We can't elect a New Yorker."

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:07.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just --
You named those critters,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.142 size:84% position:42%
and I think that was
the answer right there.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:11.611
-Hey, I want to ask about,
you know, this last year.

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it's probably
a trend over longer

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
than just a year --
don't get me wrong.

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we've seen a lot of brands
get behind social issues.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, I think
one good question --

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:25.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
if it's anything more
than just surface presentation

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.060 line:92%
of "we support these causes"

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
and maybe sort of empty tweets
and Instagram posts

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:33.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
as opposed to, you know,
actual initiatives.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:37.036
But is this -- Are you surprised
that advertising has becom

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
so tied to the social issues
of the day?

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:40.073 line:92%
-No, and they're not -- Look

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:41.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think most CEOs
are decent people,

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:43.343
but they're not necessaril
doing it from the heart.

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:44.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're doing it
from their bottom line,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.080 size:81% position:41%
in that, as you know,
every advertiser wants

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.682 align:start size:94% position:6%
18 to 34,
18, you know, to 49.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:52.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not going after
old fellas like me.

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:54.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
And young people --
that's price of entry for them

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:57.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
They want their companies
to have social consciousness

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so, you know, companie
used to stay out of it,

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:02.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
but now when it comes to thing
like basic things

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:04.931 size:75% position:38%
like racial equality
and voters' rights,

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:06.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
you better be in there,
you know?

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what's interesting,
the companies --

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:11.104
Republicans used to be friends
of companies and vice versa,

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:14.540
and now it's the Democrats
so it's interesting to see

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.211
-Would -- Do you remember
your earliest ad campaign?

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:18.878 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-My earliest ad ca--

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.447
Yeah, you know, I did a campaign
for the local Pontiac dealer

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
Remember the dealer
association ads?

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
Remember the good old guys
and things like that?

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you're from New York,
you'd know that.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Was this a case where
you were putting dealers -

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:31.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
car dealers on TV?
-Yes.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:34.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
That --
I come from humble beginnings.

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:35.395 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you can do business
with car dealers,

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:38.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can do business
with anybody.

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.333
But one of my earliest campaigns
I'm most proud of

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:41.501 size:84% position:42% line:92%
was for IKEA furniture.

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:44.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we show -- this is '93 -
a gay couple,

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:46.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
two dudes shopping
for furniture,

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:46.906 size:75% position:38% line:92%
just matter of fact.

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:50.076
And at that point, you just --
you didn't see it on television.

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:52.578
You had Billy Crystal on "Soap."
You didn't see it.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.447 size:84% position:42%
I got so many letters
from people saying just

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:56.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
thank you for just
showing guys, you know?

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.318
It was a breakthrough campaign
something I'm very proud of.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it's really nice,
as always, to have you here.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.256
Thank you for coming back
to the building for us.

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:04.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Appreciate it.
So great to be back, man.

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:07.760
-It's nice, right? It's nice
to be around human beings.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.694 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Thank you so much.

